<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 20px;">

    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Language Is an Important Skill – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Multilingual Self</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Language Is an Important Skill</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write reflective essays on personal experiences. Interactive: Write a group text on various topics, collaboratively, to be shared in a digital format.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a reflective essay about personal language learning experiences, highlighting the benefits of multilingualism (e.g., "When I started learning Guna, I felt shy to speak during family gatherings. Over time, practicing with my grandparents helped me feel more connected to my culture and community.")
                Can collaboratively write a group text about the importance of language to be shared in a digital format (e.g., "Our group believes being bilingual helps connect with more people and can create better job opportunities. For example, in Panama, many employers prefer bilingual candidates.")
                Can contribute to a shared text discussing cultural diversity (e.g., "Speaking multiple languages allows us to understand each other's cultures better. When I visited the San Blas Islands, I was able to greet locals in their language, which made the experience more meaningful.")</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Students are given a sentence stem: "I believe that learning another language is important because..." They write down three different endings to the sentence individually. Then, in pairs, they share one ending and listen to their partner's, noting down any new ideas. This activates prior knowledge about the theme "Language Is an Important Skill."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple reflective sentence using one of the endings. For example, if a student said "learning another language is important because it offers new career opportunities," the teacher would model: "My classmate, Maria, <b>said that</b> learning another language <b>offers</b> great <b>career opportunities</b>." The teacher will also highlight the use of reported speech and the target vocabulary. The teacher will explicitly mention the pronunciation of /s/ in "success" and /z/ in "citizens" while modeling related vocabulary, e.g., "success in business," "citizens of the world."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did I write what Maria said directly, or did I report what she said?" (Expected answer: Reported what she said.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of 'opportunities' did Maria mention?" (Expected answer: Career opportunities.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students read a short blog post excerpt (narrative excerpt, B1.3 register) titled "My Journey to Multilingualism," where the author reflects on their personal language learning experiences and the benefits gained. The text will incorporate target vocabulary such as <b>multilingualism</b>, <b>proficiency</b>, <b>communication</b>, <b>education</b>, <b>fluency</b>, and grammar points like <b>reported speech</b> and <b>future simple</b> for predictions about career development. It will also include pragmatic statements such as "Multilingualism allows us to connect with people from different cultures and shows respect for their background."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret the main ideas and supporting details in reflective texts about personal experiences and global issues.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What is the main topic of the blog post?" "What general benefits of multilingualism does the author discuss?" (Students write their answers in short sentences.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements about the text and mark them as True or False. E.g., "The author *said that* learning a new language was easy from the beginning." "The author *believes* that *multilingualism will enhance* future *career opportunities*."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences and short paragraphs using reported speech, future simple, modals of ability, and the target vocabulary (<b>multilingualism, language, proficiency, communication, career, opportunities, business, education, fluency, skill</b>; verbs: <b>said, argued, believe, prioritize, offer, communicate, become, excel, enhance, learn</b>; adjectives: <b>bilingual, multilingual, proficient, effective, international, diverse, multicultural, necessary, fluent</b>).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation):</b> Students are given direct quotes about language learning from various sources (e.g., "Learning a third language is hard," "Multilingualism will be a key skill for future jobs"). They rewrite these into reported speech, practicing structures like "Someone *said that* learning a third language *was* hard" or "Experts *argued that multilingualism will be* a key <b>skill</b> for future <b>jobs</b>." They also complete sentences with appropriate modals of ability and target vocabulary, e.g., "If you are <b>proficient</b> in several languages, you ______ <b>communicate</b> with people internationally." (can).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Paragraph Expansion):</b> Students receive short paragraph prompts like "Reflect on how being <b>bilingual</b> has impacted your life so far. Think about <b>communication</b> and <b>opportunities</b>." They expand these into 3-4 coherent sentences, ensuring they use at least one instance of reported speech, one future simple prediction, and two target vocabulary words. Example sentence: "My grandmother always <b>said that</b> being <b>bilingual</b> helps you connect with more people. I *believe that* it <b>will also enhance</b> my *career opportunities* in the future."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Feedback (Writing Focus):</b> In pairs, students exchange their expanded paragraphs from activity 2. They use a simple checklist to review their partner's writing for: a) Correct use of reported speech, b) Correct use of future simple, c) Inclusion of at least two target vocabulary words, d) Overall clarity and coherence. They provide constructive written feedback focusing on grammar accuracy and vocabulary usage, ensuring word stress (e.g., "multilingualism," "multicultural") is considered in their feedback if appropriate.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Multilingual Self".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative writing and individual reflective essay production.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students) to collaboratively write a short group text, and then individually write a reflective essay.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 1: Collaborative Group Text (Digital Format):</b> Each group will collaboratively write a short (5-7 sentences) paragraph or 'group text' on a shared digital document (e.g., Google Docs, shared online whiteboard) about the importance of language, specifically discussing cultural diversity and inclusiveness. They must integrate pragmatic phrases like "If we all learn even a little of each other's languages, we <b>can make</b> our community more <b>inclusive</b> and understanding." They should also use at least two target vocabulary words (e.g., <b>multicultural, communication, diversity</b>) and one instance of reported speech (e.g., "Our group *believes that* <b>multilingualism offers</b> great *opportunities* for cross-cultural *communication*").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part 2: Individual Reflective Essay:</b> Individually, students write a short reflective essay (100-120 words) about their personal language learning experiences. They should highlight the benefits of <b>multilingualism</b> and integrate a personal anecdote. They must use at least two instances of reported speech, one future simple prediction, and a minimum of five target vocabulary words. The essay should also offer practical, culturally sensitive advice, demonstrating sociolinguistic competence. For example, "When I started learning Guna, I felt shy to speak during family gatherings. My grandmother always *said that* practice is key. Now, I *believe that* this <b>skill will enhance</b> my future <b>communication</b> within my community."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment for group text, and a simple rubric for individual reflective essays.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their collaborative group text (digital) and their individual reflective essay.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Rubric for Reflective Essay and Checklist for Group Text):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Cohesion:</b> Is the writing clear and well-organized? (Reflective essay, group text)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Are at least five specified vocabulary words used appropriately? (Reflective essay) Are two specified vocabulary words used? (Group text)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of reported speech, future simple, and modals of ability. (Reflective essay, group text)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Content Relevance:</b> Does the text address the prompt (personal experience, cultural diversity, benefits of multilingualism)? (Reflective essay, group text)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Evident participation and contribution from all group members. (Group text, observed by teacher and peer input)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic/Pragmatic Awareness:</b> Does the writing promote cultural inclusiveness and offer culturally sensitive advice?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or grammar structure you used in your writing today, and how did it help you express your ideas?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did writing with your group help you understand the importance of language even better?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-8 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of writing your reflective essay or the group text, and how did you overcome it?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can the writing skills you practiced today be useful in your academic or future professional life?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the writing activities worked well, particularly in encouraging students to use reported speech, future simple, and the target vocabulary?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to further enhance their essay writing and collaborative text production, especially regarding expressing complex ideas related to multilingualism?</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to successfully integrate sociolinguistic and pragmatic functions in their writing tasks?</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will synthesize the information from their reflective essays and group texts to create a multimedia presentation promoting the benefits of multilingualism in a globalized world, demonstrating their 21st Century Skills.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>